 "educationalLevel": "International A Level",
 "hasPart": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Examine (8 marks)",
   "text": "Examine how the Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient are used to measure income inequality.",
   "answerCount": 1,
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The Lorenz curve is a graphical representation of income distribution. The horizontal axis shows the cumulative percentage of the population (from poorest to richest), and the vertical axis shows the cumulative percentage of income received. K A 45-degree line represents perfect equality — where each percentile of the population receives the same share of income. The further the Lorenz curve bows below this line, the greater the inequality . K The Gini coefficient quantifies this inequality as a single number between 0 and 1 . It is calculated as the area between the 45° line and the Lorenz curve, divided by the total area under the 45° line . K A Gini of 0 = perfect equality; a Gini of 1 = one person has all income. South Africa has a Gini of approximately 0.63, reflecting extreme inequality, while Sweden has a Gini of approximately 0.27, indicating relatively equal income distribution. A These tools are valuable for comparing inequality between countries and tracking how distribution changes over time within a country. An However, they have limitations. The Gini coefficient does not show where in the distribution inequality occurs — two countries can have the same Gini but very different patterns of inequality (e.g. one with a large middle class vs one with extremes). An Both measures also only capture income inequality , not wealth inequality, which is typically far more concentrated. An They also rely on accurate data — in countries with large informal economies, official income data may understate true inequality. An"
   }
  }
 ]
